00:12:19,246 --> 00:12:25,956
New research suggests one photo could help your doctors make health decisions, especially when it comes to cancer care.

141
00:12:26,222 --> 00:12:33,412
Researchers created an artificial intelligence health system and trained it on millions of images from a public database.

142
00:12:33,998 --> 00:12:43,556
The AI system uses software to analyze facial features like the creases around your nose that go down to your mouth, those along your forehead, and your temples.

143
00:12:44,014 --> 00:12:49,508
And researchers say these areas contain biomarkers that can help predict health outcomes.

144
00:12:50,094 --> 00:13:05,312
The AI system is then divided into two models that map these facial features, cross-reference them with age, gender, and other variables like ethnicity, and then they can make predictions on your biological age and survival rate.

145
00:13:05,392 --> 00:13:17,412
So, for example, if you wear a fitness tracker and you're into physical fitness in the app, it might say something like your cardiovascular age is younger than your actual age.

146
00:13:17,774 --> 00:13:31,812
Now apply this concept to cancer patients, and researchers say these specific facial biomarkers and the data that they get back can help make recommendations on treatment, assess potential complications, and even show survivability.

147
00:13:32,270 --> 00:13:39,872
The researchers say these models are superior to anything else on the market because of all the variables that they take into consideration.

148
00:13:40,622 --> 00:13:46,148
But the study suggests more research needs to be done before these models are rolled out.

149
00:13:46,510 --> 00:13:52,992
Now I do want to bring in cancer expert and assistant clinical professor of medicine, Dr. Imran Ali, to talk more about this.

150
00:13:53,166 --> 00:13:55,792
Dr. Ali, thank you so much for joining me again.

151
00:13:56,112 --> 00:13:56,752
Thank you, Rich.

152
00:13:57,102 --> 00:14:03,472
This is an excellent new area of artificial intelligence, especially when it comes to aging.

153
00:14:03,822 --> 00:14:12,324
So I'm a geriatrician by training, and I get a lot of, oh, let's eyeball the patient and see how old they are and see if they're frail or not.

154
00:14:12,686 --> 00:14:19,460
And the word frailty has been actually misused a lot just by eyeballing a patient.

155
00:14:19,872 --> 00:14:31,632
But this brings it to a different level because now we can use a scientific method of analyzing specific areas of the face that are associated with aging.

156
00:14:31,712 --> 00:14:34,276
And we do know that there are certain areas.

157
00:14:34,574 --> 00:14:39,392
You mentioned the creases on the forehead and then the nasolabial folds.

158
00:14:39,630 --> 00:14:51,652
We also know that, you know, what we call temporal wasting, where we have, you know, thinning of this side of the face, is often associated with muscle loss and wasting away and malnutrition.

159
00:14:52,046 --> 00:14:55,952
So there is some basis for what this model is being trained on.

160
00:14:56,270 --> 00:15:05,156
Now I was reading this on my phone like a normal millennial, and I remember that a doctor once asked me to send a picture of some stitches I had just to assess it.

161
00:15:05,486 --> 00:15:06,832
And it was fast, easy.

162
00:15:06,992 --> 00:15:09,284
It also didn't cost me an emergency room visit.

163
00:15:09,472 --> 00:15:15,012
So, from your vantage point, how advanced is this system, especially when it comes to cancer patients?

164
00:15:15,392 --> 00:15:37,284
Well, it's not ready to replace an in-person visit because to really measure somebody's physiological age, you gotta do a battery of tests, which I do as a geriatrician, something called the comprehensive geriatric assessment, where you go test the memory, the strength, the balance, and other areas which tell you if somebody's fit or frail.

165
00:15:37,646 --> 00:15:39,072
So that's the gold standard.

166
00:15:39,310 --> 00:15:47,076
But this is just something additional that we can add to our arsenal to help, you know, stratify fitness and frailty.

167
00:15:47,662 --> 00:15:57,188
You know, we've talked about AI being used in hospitals before, but if you could apply this treatment to your patients right now, how would that change your approach?

168
00:15:57,584 --> 00:16:16,452
Well, I gotta let you know that AI has been applied in in various places around the world, not necessarily the United States, but you know, for people who have dementia, who can't really talk, who are what we call aphasic, and to better understand if they're experiencing pain or discomfort.

169
00:16:16,814 --> 00:16:35,744
there are AI software programs out there that look at specific areas on a person's face to look at, you know, changes in their facial expression that are so subtle to the naked human eye that can give you an idea if it's changed from their baseline to kind of suggest if they're in discomfort.

170
00:16:36,046 --> 00:16:45,904
So this technology has been around and been used for other purposes, but now this is a totally different thing because they're using really good data.

171
00:16:45,984 --> 00:16:58,980
They looked at over 30,000 photos and they trained this foundation model with some really robust data where they looked at photos associated with side-by-side medical charts.

172
00:16:59,584 --> 00:17:04,544
And that really makes this a better foundation model because it's using real good data.

173
00:17:04,973 --> 00:17:07,588
