180
00:17:45,984 --> 00:17:49,184
This is just in addition to what your doctor is doing.

181
00:17:49,344 --> 00:17:56,624
This just helps your doctor and your team objectively analyze, you know, who you are as a person.

182
00:17:56,878 --> 00:18:01,104
So a lot of times, Rich, a lot of my patients are being written off.

183
00:18:01,264 --> 00:18:03,904
Oh, they're a little old lady, they're too frail.

184
00:18:04,110 --> 00:18:07,332
We're not gonna give them the cancer treatment because they look old.

185
00:18:07,744 --> 00:18:09,892
But necessarily that's not the case.

186
00:18:10,542 --> 00:18:27,684
You know, and the concern also from this also lies, although the research is still preliminary, is just because of a person's face ages, it doesn't necessarily tell you a whole lot about their muscle strength, their balance, their cognitive abilities.

187
00:18:28,142 --> 00:18:38,064
So while this may be something that we can add to the comprehensive assessment, it's not the panacea where we can just snap a shot and decide.

188
00:18:38,304 --> 00:18:41,184
Because my surgeon colleagues would love that.

189
00:18:41,358 --> 00:18:46,180
They would want me to give them a blood test, which has a biomarker.

190
00:18:46,478 --> 00:19:03,172
This person's too weak, this person's pretty robust, this one will go under the knife, and this one will not, you know, and we're not there, but we're working on better ways of scientifically, you know, analyzing who is fit and who is frail, who will survive and who will not survive.

191
00:19:03,790 --> 00:19:06,144
So you don't think this is going to be the end all be all, correct?

192
00:19:06,384 --> 00:19:08,624
No, it's going to be another variable.

193
00:19:08,910 --> 00:19:16,144
So when I do an assessment, I go through the 60-minute of, you know, we it's like an obstacle course, to do this, stand up, walk here.

194
00:19:16,430 --> 00:19:20,132
I time how fast they walk, and you know, I do a memory test.

195
00:19:20,494 --> 00:19:22,704
But then I also do blood tests too.

196
00:19:23,020 --> 00:19:27,172
There are certain things in the blood that are elevated in people who are older.

197
00:19:27,504 --> 00:19:35,044
This may be another thing that will be added to that whole equation that will be called you know, a frailty index.

198
00:19:35,342 --> 00:19:41,744
Now, in the comments of our last video, many people basically said hey, if there are these treatments out there, or I guess in this case, this AI model.

199
00:19:42,254 --> 00:19:46,960
And that can help patients, then why does it take so long before it ever gets used?

200
00:19:47,150 --> 00:19:48,580
What what would you say to those people?

201
00:19:49,134 --> 00:19:52,960
So the regulatory process varies from country to country.

202
00:19:53,102 --> 00:19:55,040
So I'm involved in clinical trials.

203
00:19:55,200 --> 00:20:01,092
So China has its own regulatory agency, the Europeans do, and the FDA has its own process.

204
00:20:01,486 --> 00:20:06,720
And the thing is, we want to make sure that these things that we are rolling out actually work.

205
00:20:07,086 --> 00:20:11,920
So we're not wasting your money, and that they're not harmful.

206
00:20:12,238 --> 00:20:17,600
And they're not giving you a false sense of, oh, I did this and oh, I'm fine.

207
00:20:17,806 --> 00:20:21,920
So for example, they were asking me about the blood test for colon cancer.

208
00:20:22,094 --> 00:20:24,036
I did a segment on that recently.

209
00:20:24,480 --> 00:20:32,068
And, you know, it's important to know that the blood test for colon cancer will not pick up early stages of colon cancer.

210
00:20:32,462 --> 00:20:40,640
So these are things that we only find out through intense studying, intense oversight by these regulatory agencies.

211
00:20:40,720 --> 00:20:45,520
And I know a lot of people say, well, you know, I want to be trying this new drug.

212
00:20:45,774 --> 00:20:50,628
And yes, there are exemptions in clinical trials, there's compassionate use.

213
00:20:50,894 --> 00:20:55,492
So there are ways you can get your hands on the latest treatments.

214
00:20:55,854 --> 00:21:03,684
In fact, right now, as we're filming this, you know, the European Medical Oncology Society, ESMO, is meeting in Madrid, Spain.

215
00:21:03,920 --> 00:21:06,468
A lot of cancer breakthroughs are going to be announced.

216
00:21:06,798 --> 00:21:09,120
And a lot of people are going to ask their doctors, can I try this?

217
00:21:09,200 --> 00:21:09,988
Can I try that?

218
00:21:10,446 --> 00:21:21,760
So there are a lot of things in the pipeline, but to get them out to the mainstream, we've got to make sure they actually really work in the real world, not just in the laboratory, and they're safe.

219
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:24,480
So essentially, we're hoping for hope.

220
00:21:25,006 --> 00:21:25,760
Yes, yes.

221
00:21:26,286 --> 00:21:28,000
Well, Dr. Ali, thanks so much for joining me.

222
00:21:28,080 --> 00:21:29,840
This has been a great conversation.

223
00:21:29,920 --> 00:21:34,052
And for more on this story, head over to NewsNationNow.com.

224
00:21:34,800 --> 00:21:41,924
Everything I have done in my life, educationally and professionally, has prepared me for this precise moment in time.

225
00:21:42,240 --> 00:21:49,360
We have a generational opportunity to fix our healthcare system and help people stay healthy for longer.

226
00:22:03,360 --> 00:22:09,360
This interview, as Dr. Oz just put the governors on all 50 states on notice over fraud.

227
00:22:39,360 --> 00:22:43,360
With one of the next steps being to block fraud before it even happens.

228
00:22:43,520 --> 00:22:44,000
How?

229
00:22:44,270 --> 00:22:46,692
Utilizing artificial intelligence, he says.

230
00:23:18,640 --> 00:23:20,480
Dr. Oz, great to have you with us.

231
00:23:20,640 --> 00:23:24,240
Let's jump right in and start with a headline a lot of people are tracking right now.

232
00:23:24,494 --> 00:23:28,320
President Trump's executive order to speed up research into psychedelic drugs.

233
00:23:28,400 --> 00:23:30,400
It specifically mentions ibogaine.

234
00:23:30,640 --> 00:23:35,120
Dr. Oz, in your eyes, who benefits the most from this and what do you believe is the biggest risk?

235
00:23:36,558 --> 00:23:47,456
There's lots of tantalizing evidence that altering the way people view the world around them can benefit folks with post-traumatic stress disorder, refractory depression, a whole slew of conditions.

236
00:23:47,616 --> 00:23:55,296
And ibogaine is a drug that has very low addiction potential because apparently it's such a miserable experience, you would never do it for fun.

237
00:23:55,536 --> 00:23:59,536
But it does tend to reset people in ways that may benefit them.

238
00:23:59,616 --> 00:24:04,416
It also may have some neuro-regenerative benefits, which means it might help brain cells recover.

239
00:24:04,496 --> 00:24:17,776
So if you had traumatic brain injury, which is often associated with the post-traumatic stress disorder, depression, anxiety, some of the things with that folks can be afflicted by, and maybe even with substance use disorder as a coping mechanism, it could be helpful.

240
00:24:18,062 --> 00:24:19,616
Veterans have been swearing by it.

241
00:24:19,856 --> 00:24:23,040
lots of Americans have been going overseas to get this program.

242
00:24:23,406 --> 00:24:27,696
Joe Rogan has talked about this both on a show, but also with the president.

243
00:24:27,982 --> 00:24:37,444
And as the president began to realize that not just ibogaine, but other psychedelics like MDMA, psilocybin, magic mushroom, these are drugs that have been around for a long time.

244
00:24:37,616 --> 00:24:46,096
But they do have in that case, they've been used as a street drug, but they might have therapeutic benefits when used appropriately by skilled practitioners.

245
00:24:46,176 --> 00:24:51,296
And that's why the president insisted on this executive order, which was executed last Saturday.

246
00:24:51,376 --> 00:25:00,576
And we've gotten a remarkable amount of positive feedback because these conditions are really difficult to manage, and the success of traditional pharmaceutical drugs is limited.

247
00:25:00,976 --> 00:25:05,536
Dr. Oz, with the time we have, I want to switch gears and talk about what's next for the Maha movement as well.

248
00:25:05,776 --> 00:25:08,336
the list of accomplishments already stacking up, it is admirable.

249
00:25:08,496 --> 00:25:16,336
RFK Jr. saying he wants to clean up the food supply, starting with removing hazardous synthetic food dyes, tougher scrutiny on food additives as well.

250
00:25:16,576 --> 00:25:24,896
And just this week, we see lowering prescription drug prices, a big deal with Regeneron, the 17th pharmaceutical company, to lower drug costs there.

251
00:25:25,216 --> 00:25:28,336
Now, we know not all Trump supporters are satisfied.

252
00:25:28,576 --> 00:25:30,576
The group calling themselves the Maha Moms.

253
00:25:30,926 --> 00:25:40,096
They've been really vocal in their disappointment in things like increasing the production of glyphosate, that weed killer linked with cancer, rolling back the limits on forever chemicals in our drinking water.

254
00:25:40,416 --> 00:25:43,056
Dr. Oz, do these Maha moms have a point?

255
00:25:43,216 --> 00:25:46,596
Should there be no compromise when it comes to the health of Americans?

256
00:25:47,630 --> 00:25:49,696
Well, the president is pushing for the health of Americans.

257
00:25:49,856 --> 00:26:04,016
The unfortunate reality with glyphosate, which is Roundup, is that American agriculture is addicted to it, and you need to give the farmers time to be able to move off that product into other ways of growing their crops with the same efficiency.

258
00:26:04,096 --> 00:26:06,576
And this should have been done years and years ago.

259
00:26:06,736 --> 00:26:10,496
I remember talking about these kinds of problems on my show at at least a decade ago.

260
00:26:10,576 --> 00:26:20,416
And the president finally just wrestled this down and said, for now we've got to deal with this issue that's in front of us, which is making sure that food is affordable in America and that our farmers are protected.

261
00:26:20,576 --> 00:26:25,444
But we're going to quickly, I think, over time you'll see as science now starts to focus on this problem, address it.

262
00:26:25,616 --> 00:26:29,616
But the broader narrative about Maha is about making it easier to be healthy.

263
00:26:29,776 --> 00:26:32,976
And if you look at what the administration has accomplished, it's remarkable.

264
00:26:33,216 --> 00:26:36,016
You mentioned some of the issues around things like food dyes.

265
00:26:36,206 --> 00:26:42,816
You know, obviously if the food you're about to put in your mouth has stuff that other countries don't let manufacturers use, that's a concerning issue.

266
00:26:42,976 --> 00:26:44,736
But we're also focused on plastics.

267
00:26:44,976 --> 00:27:00,816
we're looking at things just in my area of specialty, which is CMS, Medicare Medicaid, of making healthcare expenses more affordable, making sure that you have access to medications at an affordable price, so that if you do have a chronic problem like diabetes, you can take the medication that can treat your diabetes so you don't develop chronic illnesses from it.

268
00:27:00,896 --> 00:27:14,896
And you touched on it briefly, but just to underline this, pharmaceutical prices in America are so high that one in three Americans leaves the pharmacy you see right there in that image without the drugs they went there to pick up, despite the fact their doctor told them to take that medication.

269
00:27:15,056 --> 00:27:16,580
So the president said, enough's enough.

270
00:27:16,816 --> 00:27:24,656
We know we pay three times more on average than a European pays for the exact same product made often in an American facility.

271
00:27:24,816 --> 00:27:31,044
So he's insisting that we have most favored nation pricing, which means very simply, whatever you charge overseas, you charge to Americans.

272
00:27:31,246 --> 00:27:38,416
Our drug prices, therefore, some of these really important ones, like the weight loss drugs, the GLP one drugs, they now plummeted in price.

273
00:27:38,576 --> 00:27:39,696
Americans can afford them.

274
00:27:39,776 --> 00:27:56,336
And the good news is as we get them more readily priced, it actually ends up saving our country money because by making Americans healthier, by reducing, in the case of these GLP1 drugs, the amount of obesity we have in America, we have less diabetes, less hypertension, and all the complications that would have normally arisen no longer happen.

275
00:27:56,430 --> 00:27:56,976
We save money.

276
00:27:57,262 --> 00:27:58,496
Yeah, so there's a lot there.

277
00:27:58,656 --> 00:28:01,376
I want to return quickly to roundup and glyphosate.

278
00:28:01,550 --> 00:28:10,816
Do you think increasing the production of glyphosate is really about protecting the farmers, or is it evidence of how powerful lobbying is and how powerful companies like Monsanto actually are?

279
00:28:11,694 --> 00:28:16,160
It's very much about protecting the food supply in America and giving the system time to adjust.

280
00:28:16,320 --> 00:28:32,000
I I think you'll be impressed at how many really smart people are just focused in on some of the ways that Roundup are used, some of the different alternatives to that product, ways that farmers could grow crops in a similarly efficient way without being dependent on again a compound that probably should have been addressed a long time ago.

281
00:28:32,080 --> 00:28:35,120
But the president's got to deal with the issues that are in front of him.

282
00:28:35,360 --> 00:28:36,932
He marches with the army he has.

283
00:28:37,134 --> 00:28:43,600
And I am happy to say that the Maha community is strongly supportive of him as someone who's been supportive of that community for a long time.

284
00:28:43,680 --> 00:28:45,316
I feel confident in that statement.

285
00:28:45,600 --> 00:28:46,720
Let's talk about fraud.

286
00:28:46,880 --> 00:28:49,120
It seems like there is more uncovered every single week.

287
00:28:49,200 --> 00:28:55,600
I know that you're launching the fraud war room, using AI and real-time data to try to block fraud before it happens.

288
00:28:55,760 --> 00:28:57,920
It almost sounds a little like the minority report.

289
00:28:58,320 --> 00:29:07,556
tell me about, you know, how you want to use AI to fight fraud and how does one make sure that AI doesn't wrongfully freeze a legitimate doctor, hospice, or patient.

290
00:29:08,462 --> 00:29:11,440
Well, part of the goal here is to copy what's happened in the financial sector.

291
00:29:11,600 --> 00:29:12,160
Think about this.

292
00:29:12,240 --> 00:29:14,160
Bank managers are not really good at fraud.

293
00:29:14,320 --> 00:29:17,796
They wouldn't be bank managers if they were, but fraudsters, they're really good at fraud.

294
00:29:17,920 --> 00:29:24,560
So what we realize is that criminal, and this is often really well-trained foreign entities that are involved in some of this fraud.

295
00:29:24,720 --> 00:29:26,560
These are not mom and pop fraudsters.

296
00:29:26,720 --> 00:29:32,000
The corruption in the system is as bad as it is because you've got organized crime involved as well.

297
00:29:32,080 --> 00:29:38,800
And so to use an all-of-government approach, which includes, by the way, using all the tools at our fingertips like AI could be helpful.

298
00:29:38,960 --> 00:29:46,720
It worked in finance because the banks could now use AI to detect what fraudsters would do that a regular bank manager would never think they would do.

299
00:29:46,926 --> 00:29:48,080
We're using it to do the same.

300
00:29:48,240 --> 00:29:51,396
We have wonderful human beings that work at my agency, CMS.

301
00:29:51,630 --> 00:29:56,196
There are about 7,000 of us, but none of us, I don't think, went into this job to be fraudsters.

302
00:29:56,334 --> 00:30:06,160
Now AI is helping us detect subtle ways, games that, for example, we believe the Chinese mafia is using in New York State in order to take advantage of adult daycare centers.

303
00:30:06,320 --> 00:30:12,484
We know that's being used by the Russian mafia in Los Angeles, where they have one-third of all the hospices in the whole country.

304
00:30:12,750 --> 00:30:21,280
And now these big data programs can point to us exactly which of these hospices, which again, they're designed for the last few months of your life.

305
00:30:21,440 --> 00:30:23,364
What if everyone survives in the hospice?

306
00:30:23,566 --> 00:30:24,480
That's a red flag.

307
00:30:24,654 --> 00:30:30,720
What if doctors are the same ones in every one of these hospices pretending everyone's about to die?

308
00:30:30,880 --> 00:30:31,680
It's a red flag.

309
00:30:31,840 --> 00:30:39,920
So we begin to put those red flags together and using some of these larger data systems can be able to identify fraud and then go after these guys with a lot of evidence.

310
00:30:40,160 --> 00:30:45,760
So also this week, you're announcing this 50-state audit of Medicaid program oversight across the country.

311
00:30:45,920 --> 00:30:49,520
A report say that you want answers from governors within 10 days.

312
00:30:49,680 --> 00:30:51,840
What happens next if fraud is found?

313
00:30:52,000 --> 00:30:53,520
Are we talking about criminal charges?

314
00:30:53,614 --> 00:30:54,724
Are we talking about raids?

315
00:30:55,760 --> 00:30:57,360
Everything's possible.

316
00:30:57,520 --> 00:31:09,040
We saw a big opportunity in Los Angeles where as I pointed out, hospice has become such a problem with just a infrastructure that's corrupted and rotten that you couldn't tell which hospices you could trust.

317
00:31:09,200 --> 00:31:15,600
And remember, if these people will steal your money when you're in a vulnerable situation, they will definitely steal your health and your life.

318
00:31:15,760 --> 00:31:20,560
So we busted into plush neighborhoods, beautiful houses that you'd love to live in.

319
00:31:20,720 --> 00:31:23,440
And you can see this footage reflects some of these cases.

320
00:31:23,520 --> 00:31:27,200
But these individuals bought these facilities with ill-gotten gains.

321
00:31:27,280 --> 00:31:32,320
And so, yes, marching them out and stopping their businesses in an aggressive way, I think makes a lot of sense.

322
00:31:32,400 --> 00:31:35,680
But we also have ways that are more subtle but also effective.

323
00:31:35,840 --> 00:31:44,080
Like we have stopped paying more than 400 hospices in California based on the evidence that I mentioned earlier, and we're not hearing complaints, right?

324
00:31:44,480 --> 00:31:51,600
so we seem to be getting our targets appropriately identified, and when we take action of that nature, the money never leaves the building.

325
00:31:51,680 --> 00:31:52,932
And that's the big story here.

326
00:31:53,200 --> 00:31:55,600
If you can, never let the money leave the building.

327
00:31:55,822 --> 00:31:57,988
That way you don't have to go figure out where it went later on.

328
00:31:58,320 --> 00:32:00,800
We have to talk about rising health care costs as well.

329
00:32:00,960 --> 00:32:03,652
We know Affordable Care Act premiums are costs.

330
00:32:03,920 --> 00:32:06,080
They're up 60% in the past decade.

331
00:32:06,320 --> 00:32:07,600
And we crunch the numbers here.

332
00:32:07,760 --> 00:32:15,120
Americans are paying $611 today for Obamacare silver coverage that cost $381 in 2016.

333
00:32:15,280 --> 00:32:17,476
That's an extra $230 per month.

334
00:32:18,158 --> 00:32:21,380
so Dr. Oz, who is most to blame for these skyrocketing costs?

335
00:32:21,600 --> 00:32:22,800
Is it insurance companies?

336
00:32:23,022 --> 00:32:23,920
Is it big pharma?

337
00:32:24,080 --> 00:32:24,800
Is it the doctors?

338
00:32:24,960 --> 00:32:25,920
Is it politicians?

339
00:32:26,080 --> 00:32:28,560
Because Americans are telling us they can't afford it.

340
00:32:28,654 --> 00:32:33,280
And if you could do one thing to fix these skyrocketing costs, what would you do?

341
00:32:34,286 --> 00:32:36,368
Well, in the case of the ACA, it's a team effort.

342
00:32:36,528 --> 00:32:39,968
Every one of the people you mentioned plays a role in it.

343
00:32:40,048 --> 00:32:46,628
But fundamentally in the ACA, the Obamacare, there was a willingness to just pay whatever was being asked.

344
00:32:46,926 --> 00:32:54,756
And so when you throw money at a problem, people running the system will take your money and they'll make sure that the charges match what you just paid them.

345
00:32:54,848 --> 00:32:57,248
And we saw that happening in many different situations.

346
00:32:57,488 --> 00:33:00,368
We still believe there's a lot of fraud in the ACA.

347
00:33:00,448 --> 00:33:14,528
I mean, millions and millions of people are not supposed to be on the Affordable Care Act, Obamacare exchanges, but they're either put there by mistake or they don't know they're there, or fraudsters, and we're talking about people making hundreds of millions of dollars off the system.

348
00:33:14,780 --> 00:33:18,128
dishonestly pretend that you're eligible for Obamacare when you're not.

349
00:33:18,208 --> 00:33:18,528
That's the way that's the right thing.

350
00:33:19,248 --> 00:33:20,528
I hear the fraud piece of it.

351
00:33:20,654 --> 00:33:24,132
If you could do one thing, no pushback to fix this, what would you do?

352
00:33:24,974 --> 00:33:29,316
I would ask everyone in Obamacare to pay five Why?

353
00:33:29,582 --> 00:33:32,068
Because if you pay five I know you're real.

354
00:33:32,430 --> 00:33:38,608
You're not a fiction made up person, you're not someone whose number was stolen and illegally placed on the program.

355
00:33:39,728 --> 00:33:44,368
you actually want to be on the program, you're not already on Medicaid or living in a different state.

356
00:33:44,528 --> 00:33:51,088
And when you look at these numbers, these are tens of billions of dollars being lost because we don't even know that you're supposed to be on the program.

357
00:33:51,248 --> 00:33:54,048
The people who need to be on the Obamacare system should be on it.

358
00:33:54,318 --> 00:33:59,648
Folks who have left it for good reason or other, they should be not being paid for by the American taxpayer.

359
00:33:59,982 --> 00:34:00,128
Dr.

360
00:34:00,128 --> 00:34:01,568
Oz, appreciate your time today.

361
00:34:01,648 --> 00:34:02,052
Thank you.

362
00:34:03,328 --> 00:34:04,208
Thank you for watching.

363
00:34:04,368 --> 00:34:13,060
